---
trace: christianjmills/procedural-map-generation-techniques-notes
---
Fill a grid with random rock, then apply one rule to every tile, again and again {% ref source="this" path="assets/caves.js" lines="52-56" /%}. The talk's slide puts the recipe in three steps: make a random map, apply cell life rules to each tile, repeat {% cite id="wolverson2020-slides/pdf#p9" /%}. Herbert Wolverson's talk on procedural map generation introduces cellular automata as a way to evolve an ordered, natural-looking map from complete chaos {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s73" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s74" /%}. Unlike some of the talk's other techniques, this one really does start from noise: half the map walls and half floors, picked at random, with no way through {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s74" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s75" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s76" /%}. The talk likens it to Conway's Game of Life {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s77" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s78" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-slides/pdf#p9" /%}. This page builds that loop and lets you step through it.

## The rule

Each pass works from a copy of the map: it visits every tile that isn't on the edge and counts its neighbours, diagonals included {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s79" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s80" /%}. A tile with no neighbours becomes a wall, one with one to four becomes empty, and one with five or more becomes a wall {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s80" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s81" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s82" /%}. Wolverson adds that you can and should tweak these rules to suit your game {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s82" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s83" /%}.

The neighbours counted are walls, as the talk's code shows further down. This page's version states the same rule in terms of rock: a tile becomes rock if more than four of its eight neighbours are rock, or if none of them are, and open floor otherwise. Each generation reads the previous grid and writes a new one, so every tile sees the same moment {% ref source="this" path="assets/caves.js" lines="40-50" /%}:

```javascript
export function step(grid, width, height) {
  const out = grid.slice();
  for (let y = 1; y < height - 1; y++) {
    for (let x = 1; x < width - 1; x++) {
      const n = rockNeighbours(grid, width, x, y);
      out[y * width + x] = n > 4 || n === 0 ? 1 : 0;
    }
  }
  return out;
}
```

The first half of the rule does the smoothing. A tile in a mostly rocky neighbourhood fills in and a tile in a mostly open one clears, so the noise clumps into solid walls and open space {% own /%}. The second half, rock when no neighbour is rock, puts single-tile pillars in the middle of wide-open areas {% own /%}.

The rule ignores a tile's own state: only its neighbours count {% ref source="this" path="assets/caves.js" lines="30-38" /%}.

## Try it

{% interactive id="caves" caption="An 80 × 50 cave map. Change the starting rock, step through the generations, or try another seed." %}
{% figure src="assets/caves-fallback.png" alt="Three 80 by 50 tile maps side by side, all from seed 1 with 55% starting rock. Left, generation 0: evenly scattered noise. Middle, generation 3: the noise has clumped into connected open areas bounded by rock. Right, generation 10: smoother caves with a few single-tile pillars in the open areas." caption="Without JavaScript: seed 1 with 55% starting rock, at generations 0, 3 and 10." /%}

At seed 1 with 55% starting rock, 41.6% of the map is open at generation 0, 48.8% after three generations and 52.1% after ten {% own-run values="open.g0 open.g3 open.g10" /%}. The outer ring is always rock, which counts toward the starting share {% ref source="this" path="assets/caves.js" lines="17-28" /%}.
{% /interactive %}

The talk then shows these steps animated: the random soup quickly turns into a far more natural-looking structure that can be made into a useful map {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s83" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s84" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-slides/pdf#p10" /%}. Wolverson calls it a really simple, really fast algorithm that gives the same result every time from the same seed {% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s85" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-talk/transcript#s86" /%}.

## It never settles

Step past the third generation and the caves' overall shape holds, but tiles never stop flipping. At 55% starting rock, about 1,040 of the 4,000 tiles still change between generations 9 and 10, averaged over seeds 1 to 50 {% own-run values="churn.g10" /%}. Without the "none are rock" half of the rule, far fewer flip: at seed 1, 200 tiles change at generation 10 {% own-run values="churn.nozero.seed1" /%}. The talk's demo runs a fixed ten iterations {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c03-cellular/src/main.rs#L31-L34" /%}, so when to stop is a choice, not something the rule decides {% own /%}.

The starting share decides whether the caves connect. Over the same 50 seeds after ten generations, the largest open region, counting tiles joined edge to edge, holds 98% of the floor at 55% starting rock. At 60% it holds 63%, spread over about 10 regions. At 65% it holds 31%, over about 15 {% own-run values="largest.55 largest.60 regions.60 largest.65 regions.65" /%}.

## Reading the talk's code

The talk's demo runs the same loop on an 80 × 50 map: random tiles, 55% of them `.`, then ten iterations, each one recorded as a frame {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/backend/src/lib.rs#L3-L4" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c03-cellular/src/main.rs#L17-L34" /%}. Its rule is written in terms of `.` tiles: count the `.` neighbours; a tile with none becomes `.`, a tile with fewer than five becomes `#`, and any other tile becomes `.` {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c03-cellular/src/main.rs#L40-L54" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c03-cellular/src/main.rs#L56-L70" /%}.

That looks like the opposite of the talk's rule until you check which glyph is the wall {% own /%}. A new map is all `.` {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/backend/src/lib.rs#L12-L16" /%}, the shared map code lets a tile lead only to neighbours that are not `.` {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/backend/src/lib.rs#L47-L68" /%}, and the demo labels its first frame "Random Noise - 55% Walls" {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c03-cellular/src/main.rs#L19-L29" /%}. So `.` is the wall and `#` is the floor, and the demo's rule is the talk's rule {% own /%}.

{% callout type="note" title="Where this version differs" %}
The talk's demo rolls every tile at random, the outer ring included, and then only ever updates interior tiles, so the outer ring keeps its random roll {% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c03-cellular/src/main.rs#L17-L34" /%}{% cite id="wolverson2020-code/c03-cellular/src/main.rs#L56-L70" /%}. This version makes the outer ring rock before the first generation, so the caves are always closed {% ref source="this" path="assets/caves.js" lines="17-28" /%}.
{% /callout %}

## What it doesn't do

The rule says nothing about connectivity {% own /%}. Even at 55% starting rock, 49 of the 50 seeds leave at least one pocket of floor that the largest cave doesn't reach after ten generations {% own-run values="split.55" /%}. A game has to fill those pockets or tunnel to them before it places anything there {% own /%}.
